1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,120
The Personal Protective Equipment, PPE, worn by actors in this video was selected

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:10,080
based on a risk assessment of the chemicals used in the experiment and conditions simulated

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:14,960
in the activity. In scenes where the presenter did not wear PPE while in the lab,

4
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:18,000
all chemicals were removed to eliminate chemical hazards.

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:25,380
The ability to recognize hazards is important for designing and carrying out safe lab experiments.

6
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:29,540
In this video, you'll learn to recognize chemical hazards using written sources of information.

7
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:35,620
Recognizing hazards is part of a larger process for evaluating safe lab procedures called RAMP.

8
00:00:36,340 --> 00:00:41,780
RAMP stands for Recognize Hazards, Assess Risks, Minimize Risks, and Prepare for Emergencies.

9
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:46,180
Each experiment or process presents its own set of hazards.

10
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:50,660
All hazards need to be recognized so their risk can be assessed and minimized.

11
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:54,980
This video will focus on hazards from chemicals, but there are other hazards associated with lab

12
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:59,140
works such as high pressure, low and high temperatures, and electrical hazards.

13
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:06,300
In the United States, chemical manufacturers are required to supply a Safety Data Sheet,

14
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:12,060
or SDS, with information about their chemicals. The SDS follows the United Nations' globally

15
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:17,500
harmonized system of classification and labeling of chemicals, usually referred to as the GHS.

16
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:22,780
The SDS is an important document and crucial for your safety in the lab.

17
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:26,860
It's the first place you should go to to determine the hazards of chemicals you'll be working with.

18
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:33,820
All SDSs have the same 16-section format, but we'll only look at the 7 sections related to

19
00:01:33,820 --> 00:01:39,100
recognizing hazards. Now let's take a closer look at the SDS for some chemicals commonly used in the

20
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:44,140
lab. Section 1 identifies the chemical. It's crucial you have the SDS for the right one.

21
00:01:45,020 --> 00:01:48,620
Chemicals sometimes have several names and these will be listed on the SDS.

22
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:53,900
Also make sure the concentration is the same. If you're working with glacial acetic acid, which

23
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:59,340
contains 99% acetic acid, you don't want an SDS for vinegar, which only contains 4%.

24
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:07,020
Finally, every chemical has a unique chemical abstract service registry number. This identifier

25
00:02:07,020 --> 00:02:10,060
and the name on the container must match the number on the SDS.

26
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:15,900
Section 2 lists hazards for chemicals in several ways. The most direct way is a list of hazard

27
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:21,100
statements. There are 78 official hazard statements in the GHS. Most chemicals only have

28
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:26,540
a few of these. Every SDS will also have pictograms, symbols representing different

29
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:31,100
categories and hazards. Each pictogram represents a specific type of hazard.

30
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:36,300
There are 9 pictograms. Most chemicals have only 1 or 2 pictograms.

31
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:41,900
The hazards of ethyl ether, for example, can be immediately recognized in any language

32
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:46,540
with the 3 pictograms, flammability, irritant, and health hazard.

33
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:53,580
Section 2 also provides a signal word, either warning or danger. This gives us a rough estimate

34
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:59,500
of the relative degree of hazard. Finally, Section 2 also lists precautionary statements.

35
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:04,140
These provide advice for using the chemical. You can see that ethyl ether has a long list

36
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:09,740
of these statements. Section 4 provides first aid information for each way a chemical might enter

37
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:16,460
the body. These typically include the eyes, nose, mouth, and skin. You should consider these

38
00:03:16,460 --> 00:03:21,740
routes of entry when thinking about how a chemical could harm you. Section 8 includes exposure control

39
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:27,820
terms such as TWA and PEL, which stand for time-weighted average and permissible exposure

40
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:33,580
limit. Generally, low numbers here mean serious inhalation hazards. For comparison, carbon monoxide

41
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:38,940
has a TWA of 25 parts per million, while ethanol has a TWA of 1000 parts per million.

42
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:43,580
This means you need to be more careful about exposure to carbon monoxide than ethanol.

43
00:03:44,380 --> 00:03:49,420
Section 9 summarizes physical and chemical properties. The boiling point and flash point

44
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:54,300
are used to determine flammability. For example, the flash point for ethyl ether is quite low at

45
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:59,420
negative 45 degrees Celsius. This indicates that vapor easily forms and could ignite at room

46
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:04,220
temperature. Section 10 tells us about the stability and reactivity of the chemical.

47
00:04:04,220 --> 00:04:10,380
For example, nitric acid is very reactive and has a long list of known incompatible chemicals.

48
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:14,940
Incompatables are listed in this section. Pay special attention to this section when disposing

49
00:04:14,940 --> 00:04:19,740
of waste. Many lab incidents are caused by accidentally combining incompatible chemicals

50
00:04:19,740 --> 00:04:24,780
in the same waste bottle. The SCS for ethyl ether says it shouldn't be mixed with oxidizing

51
00:04:24,780 --> 00:04:30,620
agents or strong acids such as nitric acid. Section 11 provides info about the toxicity of

52
00:04:30,620 --> 00:04:36,140
a chemical, the dose that can harm you. This is often expressed as lethal dose, LD50,

53
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:42,380
or lethal concentration, LC50. The number 50 tells us how much of a chemical it took to kill

54
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:48,140
50% of a test population of lab animals. The toxicity of a chemical is divided into

55
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:53,420
acute or short-term effects and chronic or long-term effects. We worry mostly about

56
00:04:53,420 --> 00:04:58,700
short-term effects in the lab. With ether, a single exposure may cause drowsiness or dizziness.

57
00:04:58,700 --> 00:05:08,900
In fact, it was once used as an anesthetic for surgery. Overall, SDSs are useful, but they

58
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:13,540
aren't always the best source of safety information. This is partly because SDSs are

59
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:17,940
written for a variety of audiences, so some information is not very relevant in the lab.

60
00:05:18,580 --> 00:05:24,100
In particular, pay attention to the terms no data available, Nslash A, and not established.

61
00:05:24,980 --> 00:05:29,060
If you encounter the phrase no data available, it should be viewed with some caution.

62
00:05:29,780 --> 00:05:33,940
It could mean data hasn't been reported for the property of the chemical. After all,

63
00:05:33,940 --> 00:05:38,100
there are many tens of thousands of chemicals in use, but only a small percentage have been tested

64
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:43,300
for toxicity. Or it could be the person who wrote the SDS simply didn't include information

65
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:48,340
that is readily available. Above all else, you shouldn't interpret no data to mean this

66
00:05:48,340 --> 00:05:53,540
chemical is safe. It just means we don't know or we didn't look. For common chemicals,

67
00:05:53,540 --> 00:05:58,340
there shouldn't be many of these no data entries. Not applicable is used for properties

68
00:05:58,340 --> 00:06:03,140
which don't apply to the chemical in its specified form. For example, you wouldn't expect to see a

69
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:09,140
melting point or freezing point for a gas. If you see Nslash A, it can mean either

70
00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:15,620
not applicable or not available. Here are a few other concerns about SDSs. For one,

71
00:06:15,620 --> 00:06:20,660
they are not legally required to be accurate. Studies have shown that some SDSs have errors

72
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:26,660
and omissions. Second, SDSs for the same chemical can vary between manufacturers.

73
00:06:26,660 --> 00:06:31,860
And finally, sometimes they present accurate but not very helpful statements like dispose of

74
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:36,740
waste according to local regulations. If you can't find what you need in an SDS,

75
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:39,860
there are other sources of useful info about chemical hazards.

76
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:46,580
First, there's the PubChem Laboratory Chemical Safety Summary or LCSS. These are similar to

77
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:52,100
the SDS but use different section numbers and advantage of the LCSS as it shows combined

78
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:57,380
information for many sources. Another resource is the National Fire Protection Association

79
00:06:57,380 --> 00:07:02,020
Fire Hazard Diamond. Numbers from 0 to 4 indicate hazards for firefighters.

80
00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:09,380
0 means non-hazardous and 4 means very hazardous. Colors define the type of hazard. Red indicates

81
00:07:09,380 --> 00:07:15,380
flammability. Blue a health hazard. Yellow an instability hazard and white is for special

82
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:21,380
instructions such as OX for oxidizers present. Keep in mind that information resources can

83
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:26,660
only state hazards, not assess risks. You will always have to assess the risk of using certain

84
00:07:26,660 --> 00:07:32,180
lab chemicals. Depending on how much of a chemical is used and how it is used, even a very hazardous

85
00:07:32,180 --> 00:07:38,820
chemical can present little risk. Remember RAMP, the four-step process we mentioned at the beginning

86
00:07:38,820 --> 00:07:44,340
of this video? Recognizing chemical hazards is an important part of this process. Over time,

87
00:07:44,340 --> 00:07:49,140
you'll learn how to read an SDS with a critical eye so you can use chemicals safely but with

88
00:07:49,140 --> 00:07:53,860
reasonable precautions. To learn more about the other steps in RAMP, be sure to check out the other

89
00:07:53,860 --> 00:07:58,580
five videos in this series. You can find them by clicking here, checking out the links in the

90
